WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.380
This episode of Real Science is brought
to you by Curiosity Stream. Watch

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:09.690
thousands of documentaries for free for
31 days at curiositystream.com/realscience

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:15.150
It's been all over the news
lately. There have been a record-breaking

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.439
number of fires ravaging the Brazilian
Amazon rainforest this year - manmade

00:00:19.439 --> 00:00:21.840
fires,
intentionally set to clear land for

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.220
agriculture, which then spread
uncontrollably. The National Institute

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.570
for Space Research says it has detected
more than 74 thousand fires between

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:35.070
January and August. In comparison there
were fewer than 40,000 for the same

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:39.989
period in 2018. It is shocking to see it
happening to a place many of us consider

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:44.879
to be a pristine lush expanse. And most
of us looking on from thousands of miles

00:00:44.879 --> 00:00:49.530
away from behind our computer screens
feel helpless. Members of the G7 summit

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:54.090
pledged 22 million dollars to help fight
the fires. And over the past decade

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:58.829
Norway has donated 1.2 billion dollars
to help conserve the Amazon and Germany

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:02.940
has contributed 68 million dollars.
However they've both stopped their

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.900
contributions because of doubts over
Brazil's efforts to reduce deforestation.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.729
Despite money pouring in over many years
to try to battle the problem,

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:15.840
international campaigns, summits, and
boycotts, it just isn't working. The

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.680
recent fires are just a symptom of an
ongoing problem of unregulated and

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.360
out-of-control clearing for agriculture.
Across the world, in South America, Africa,

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:29.159
and Asia, the world's rainforests are
being lost at a rapid pace. If current

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:33.479
deforestation levels precede, the world's
rainforests may completely vanish in as

00:01:33.479 --> 00:01:38.369
little as a hundred years. And there is
so much to lose. The diversity of plants

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:40.710
and animals in the world's rainforests
is staggering,

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.579
especially in person. Seeing mist rise
above the canopy at sunrise while

00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:48.119
hearing Gibbon calls from miles away,
trekking through the forest at night and

00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:51.600
seeing alien like insects,
dodging snakes wrapped around branches,

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.009
colorful ridiculous birds. The amount of
amazing creatures there is seemingly

00:01:56.009 --> 00:01:58.539
endless.
And the value that they have to the

00:01:58.539 --> 00:02:03.100
world is impossible to quantify. And so,
with all the terrible news stories we

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.420
hear it's easy to feel like there is no
hope. To see any animals losing their

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:13.269
habitat is heartbreaking. However, despite
the headlines, all hope is not lost. There

00:02:13.269 --> 00:02:16.480
are things that can be done and people
who are doing everything in their power

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.129
to ensure the rainforests survive the
recent onslaught of destruction. To

00:02:21.129 --> 00:02:24.549
understand what is being done and how,
let's focus on one of the most

00:02:24.549 --> 00:02:29.440
endangered ecosystems in the world, the
rainforests of Malaysia and Indonesia, and in

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.150
particular Borneo - home to recognizable
species such as orangutangs and pygmy

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:38.410
elephant. Borneo's natural forests
continue to be destroyed at a rapid pace.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:44.799
Between 1985 and 2005 Borneo lost an
average of 850 thousand hectares of

00:02:44.799 --> 00:02:49.239
forest every year. If this trend
continues, forest cover will drop to less

00:02:49.239 --> 00:02:53.560
than a third in coming years. And all
that deforestation has been devastating

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:57.730
for wildlife. Nearly a hundred and fifty
thousand critically endangered Bornean

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:03.459
orangutans died between 1999 and 2015 and there are less than 1500 surviving

00:03:03.459 --> 00:03:07.660
Borneo pygmy elephants. 10 to 15 percent
of the world's unique plant and animal

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:11.440
species live in this area and they are
all at risk of being wiped out entirely.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.950
And this is not to mention the
importance of the forest and its carbon

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:18.459
storing abilities and how devastating
clearing it will be in the battle

00:03:18.459 --> 00:03:22.690
against climate change. It's easy to
point out why deforestation is bad for

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:26.709
biodiversity, and no one wants the
orangutans to die, so why is it happening?

00:03:26.709 --> 00:03:31.209
The answer to this question varies from
region to region and from year to year.

00:03:31.209 --> 00:03:36.639
In Brazil, cattle ranching is the leading
cause. In the DRC it's largely due to

00:03:36.639 --> 00:03:41.169
unregulated or illegal logging for
timber. And in Borneo and the rest of

00:03:41.169 --> 00:03:44.590
Indonesia and Malaysia, the forests are
currently being cleared to make room for

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.959
the most in-demand plant oil in the
world -

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:51.970
palm oil. this huge demand for palm oil
can be traced back to the 1980s when the

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:56.019
world learned the dangers of trans fats.
Food producers scrambled to find a

00:03:56.019 --> 00:03:59.950
suitable alternative - something cheap
with similar properties to trans fats

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:04.419
but less damaging to human health. The
substance that ticked these three boxes

00:04:04.419 --> 00:04:08.880
was you guessed it, palm oil.
Then in the last few decades

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.270
environmentalists ironically pushed for
an increase in biodiesel production to

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.780
try to stop the release of carbon from
fossil fuels, not foreseeing the

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:21.489
contradiction in their thinking. In the
US a law mandating that biofuels be

00:04:21.489 --> 00:04:25.930
incorporated into diesel promised to stop
the release of 4.5 billion tons of

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:30.580
carbon over three decades. Biodiesel
production in the U.S. jumped from 250

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:36.760
million gallons in 2006 to more than 1.5
billion gallons in 2016. Like many

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:40.630
well-intentioned plans this one did the
exact opposite of what it was supposed

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:44.350
to and helped lead to the ongoing
decimation of one of the world's biggest

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:49.120
carbon sinks. By the 21st century the
palm oil boom was in full swing and

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:53.020
thousands of square miles of lowland
forests across Borneo were planted with

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:58.540
oil palms. Today Indonesia and Malaysia
supply 85% of the world's palm oil, and

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:02.050
whether we realize it or not palm oil is
in everything -

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:07.840
pizza dough, lipstick, ice cream, laundry
detergent, soap, chocolate, instant noodles,

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.680
fuel. It's an extremely cheap and
versatile product and it's nearly

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.150
impossible to avoid in modern life.
Globally we each consume an average of 8

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:20.440
kilograms of palm oil a year. When you're
in Borneo you can drive for hours and

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:25.060
hours and hours and see nothing but palm
trees. And much of the forest you can see

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.780
is under severe pressure with animals
being forced into smaller and smaller

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:32.830
habitats. Trucks carrying the palm
kernels and trucks lugging out massive

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:37.060
ancient trees zigzag across the
landscape. This leads to the hardest

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:41.290
question of it all. What can be done
about it? There's been a recent push to

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:44.950
boycott palm oil products, like the
grocery chain Iceland is doing as

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:48.940
explained in their controversial viral
ad. This kind of stuff feels nice and is

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:52.780
easy for people to support. However,
Europe and the U.S. account for less than

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:57.190
14 percent of global palm oil demand. So
boycotts coming from these parts of the

00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:02.080
world are unfortunately not enough. Half
of global demand comes from Asia where

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.280
within many of the developing economies
product price is what matters. And with

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:10.990
palm oil accounting for 13.7 percent of
Malaysia's gross national income and

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:15.550
existing as Indonesia's top export,
outright banning its cultivation is just

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:19.720
not going to happen. The way to actually
save the rainforests is unfortunately

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:23.140
way more
complicated. To save the rainforest we

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:26.380
have to understand the rainforest. We
have to understand how it actually

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:31.660
reacts to deforestation and degradation.
Obviously when an entire forest slashed

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:35.620
and burned and logged, that ecosystem is
lost. But what about the surrounding

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:39.730
forests, are they impacted by the
adjacent damage? Are any animals able to

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:44.770
relocate? And if so, what kinds, how many?
What about partially degraded forests, or

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:48.850
young forests that are being restored?
Can they support wildlife? Can some

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:52.840
animals actually even live within the
palm oil plantations? Are wildlife

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:56.020
corridors along rivers enough to promote
the movement of animals across a

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:00.430
plantation landscape? How much forest can we actually lose before the damage is

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:04.450
irreversible? These are the questions
that need to be answered to ensure the

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:08.680
future of the Southeast Asian rainforest,
and in fact any threatened forest in the

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:12.940
world. One group working on finding the
answers to these questions is a group of

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:16.900
scientists nestled deep in the heart of
Malaysian Borneo at the SAFE project

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:21.669
site. SAFE stands for the stability of
altered forest ecosystems and their goal

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:25.960
is to research biodiversity and
ecosystem function change as forests are

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:29.650
modified by human activities and to
learn whether preserving sections of

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:33.520
forests within degraded landscapes can
protect biodiversity, and how much

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:38.080
protection is needed to be effective. The
entire SAFE project experimental site

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.700
has an area of 72 square kilometers
which is spread over existing palm oil

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:46.990
plantations and untouched rainforest,
much of which is slated to soon be

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:51.640
converted into palm oil plantations. The
site also contains a large virgin jungle

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:55.630
reserve of 22 square kilometers which
will remain protected throughout the

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:59.680
process. Within the total safe project
area the owners of the palm oil

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.070
plantation have agreed to allow an
additional 8 square kilometres of land

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:07.030
to be set aside as forest fragments.
These will be the experimental forest

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:10.690
fragments that the scientists can study.
In addition to this Malaysian law

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:15.100
prohibits the clearance of forests on
steep slopes and along rivers accounting

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:18.820
for another approximately five square
kilometres. With this arrangement the

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:22.750
scientists will be able to study the
effect of logging before during and

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:26.350
after such forest conversion and can
also study the effects of forest

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:30.880
corridors and reserves within damaged
forests. This type of experimental design

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:34.770
is extremely valuable and
extremely rare. It is not often that

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:38.040
scientists get to work with the
cooperation of the very people who are

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:41.280
doing the damage that is being studied
and get to choose where their

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:45.690
experimental sites are. In most cases
ecological research is carried out

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:49.980
observationally, after the fact, which
does not produce as powerful of results

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:54.090
as an experiment like this will. The
overall goal of the safe project is to

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:58.350
determine the minimum critical size
forest fragments can be before they fail

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:02.370
to operate as functional tropical
ecosystems. They are gathering data on

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:07.560
animal populations, soil composition,
plant populations, hydrology, insect

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:12.000
behavior, seed dispersal, everything that
a healthy tropical ecosystem needs and

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.810
seeing how that changes in different
levels of forest destruction. And with

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:19.890
this information the ultimate goal is to
find out the best way to sustainably

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:24.210
farm palm oil - to find a compromise
between agriculture and conservation. And

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:28.560
while this experiment is set to go on
for many years to come and the full

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.940
results won't be known until then, there
are already hundreds of papers pouring

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:36.150
out of this research site and some of
the results already have big

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:40.860
implications. One study for example found
that riparian reserves, the strips of

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:44.520
forests that are protected along the
lengths of rivers, that have over 40

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:48.870
metres of natural vegetation on each
bank supported similar bird diversity to

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:53.910
the control habitats found in continuous
protected forests. However to support

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:57.750
equivalent numbers of birds of
conservation concern, reserves would need

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:01.590
to be at least a hundred metres wide on
each bank. Another study concluded that

00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:05.790
over all mammal species richness was
conserved even in degraded forests,

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:09.570
forests that otherwise might be thought
to be too damaged already to be worth

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:14.310
protecting. And yet another study found
unexpectedly that there is no impact

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:17.940
from land use changes on the biomass or
number of fish and small streams,

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:21.960
suggesting that these fish could be a
sustainable food source for villages

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:26.880
established in human modified forests or
in developed oil palm plantations. These

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.900
are the types of results that SAFE wants
to use to inform how palm oil is farmed

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:35.160
and in fact how any fragmented landscape
can be designed to best preserve the

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:38.730
environment in the face of land-use
change. And once even more years of data

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:42.210
have been collected the SAFE project
will inform relevant governments about

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:45.410
the best land-use policy. For example, at
the moment

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:49.040
governmental guidelines on the amount of
riparian reserves around rivers varies

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:53.750
greatly. In Sabah, the law says that 20
meters of natural vegetation on either

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:58.699
side of a riverbank has to be preserved.
In Indonesia it's 50 meters, in other

00:10:58.699 --> 00:11:01.970
parts of Malaysia it's 5 meters, and
other regions have different guidelines

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:06.500
altogether. But as I mentioned before
some studies are already finding that

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:10.009
maybe a hundred meters or more are
necessary to be sufficient wildlife

00:11:10.009 --> 00:11:13.910
corridors to preserve biodiversity.
Results like these will be funneled to

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:18.259
policymakers and members of the round
table for sustainable palm oil to try to

00:11:18.259 --> 00:11:22.189
set up a system in which despite some
deforestation happening now, the

00:11:22.189 --> 00:11:26.240
rainforest can survive and rebound once
the local economies move on to other

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:30.350
forms ofiIndustry one day in the future.
To really save the rainforest requires

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:35.360
an unromantic often tedious compromise
between industry, ecology, and politics.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:39.019
None of it is easy, and with fairly
rampant corruption in many of the

00:11:39.019 --> 00:11:42.949
countries in question, undermining any
progress made, it can feel like an

00:11:42.949 --> 00:11:47.029
impossible battle. It will take years of
discussion from the decision-makers, long

00:11:47.029 --> 00:11:51.380
hours spent in the field, and integrity
from relevant politicians. But with all

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:55.250
of these things working together, in
theory, there is hope for a future world

00:11:55.250 --> 00:12:00.439
still covered in rainforests. The subject
of deforestation is a massive one and

00:12:00.439 --> 00:12:04.370
there are many many things we need to
understand in order to save the world's

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:08.180
forests. Scientists everywhere are
tirelessly gathering data and

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:12.470
discovering the answers needed to inform
conservation efforts. And while they are

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:16.519
working to understand the rainforest, we
can work to understand their efforts and

00:12:16.519 --> 00:12:20.290
the ecology that surrounds the problem
by signing up to Curiosity Stream.

